#---
# purpose: "a guard whose sum leaves the 64-bit integer does not match its clause, in an entry guard and in a receive guard, where a body raises"
#---
#
# Integers are 64 bits wide, so `n + 1` at the largest integer fails the way
# the same sum in a body does. In a guard that failure is the clause not
# matching. Elixir has unbounded integers and answers `:a` for the largest
# integer, so this fixture has no Elixir twin.
def entry(n) when n + 1 > 0, do: :a
def entry(_), do: :b

def receive_sum(m) do
  send(self(), m)
  receive do
    n when n + 1 > 0 -> :a
    _ -> :b
  end
end

def main() do
  largest = 9223372036854775807
  dbg([entry(largest), entry(1), receive_sum(largest), receive_sum(1)])
end
